      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's part 4 of 4 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 15, project number 4076767, pages 11-13 of 13.\n\nContent present:\n1. Certificate header info: Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 15, Project number 4076767, page total 13, member state Netherlands, issuing authority NMi (implied by \"NMi\" in header), OIML Member State: The Netherlands.\n\n2. Tables 9-12: General characteristics of the indicating device, by meter size, for four configurations:\n   - Table 9: Reduced bore – AC mains powered\n   - Table 10: Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\n   - Table 11: Full bore – AC mains powered\n   - Table 12: Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered (spans pages 12-13)\n\n3. Production locations (page 13): four ABB entities.\n\n4. Certificate history: revisions Initial through 15 with dates and changes.\n\nThe certificate number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 → scheme A, recommendation R49 (2013 edition). Wait — the number format \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — the recommendation is R49 (water meters), edition 2013, scheme A. Accuracy classes mentioned in revision history: accuracy class 1 (for DN1800), also E2 class and O class for flow transmitter (those are meter classes per R49? Actually R49 water meters have accuracy classes 1 and 2, and meter classes... Hmm, \"E2 class and O class\" — those might be environmental/electromagnetic classes? Actually in R49, there are accuracy classes 1 and 2 and metrological classes... Actually \"class E2\" and \"class O\" — hmm. Not sure. I'll include accuracy_classes: [1, 2]? Only class 1 is mentioned. Actually accuracy class 2 would presumably be present from the start, but only \"accuracy class 1\" is mentioned in revision 6. Don't fabricate — I'll include accuracy_classes: [\"1\"] maybe. Hmm, but the schema says recommendation: id, edition (int), amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]. R49 edition 2013, scheme A. Accuracy classes — only class 1 is explicitly mentioned; I could include [\"1\"] but the full cert likely covers classes 1 and 2. Since we only have this fragment, mention [\"1\"]? The instruction: \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" Accuracy class 1 is mentioned. I'll include [\"1\"] — wait but that could be misleading as the cert likely covers both classes. But based on fragment, only class 1 is explicitly mentioned. Hmm. I think it's safer to include accuracy_classes: [\"1\"] since that's what's in the fragment. Actually, accuracy_classes should reflect the certificate's accuracy classes. The fragment only evidences class 1. I'll include [\"1\"].\n\nEdition: The number is \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". The \"2013\" is the edition year of the recommendation OIML R 49 (2013)? Actually R49 is \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot potable water\" — editions: 2013? Actually R49-1:2013? Hmm, OIML R49 parts were published in 2013? No — R49-1/2/3 are dated 2013? Let me think. OIML R 49 was published 2013? Actually OIML R 49-1:2013, R 49-2:2013, R 49-3:2013 — yes, edition 2013. So edition: 2013.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — hmm wait, typically NMi certs like \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — scheme A. So certificate.scheme = \"A\". Also recommendation scheme = \"A\" (A means OIML certificate for type evaluation without mutual acceptance? Actually scheme A = \"OIML Certificate of Conformity\" based on national type evaluation; scheme B = MAA). Include both.\n\nPage total: 13. Pages in this fragment: 11, 12, 13.\n\nIssuing authority: NMi (Nederlandse Meetinstantie). Name: \"NMi\" per header. The fragment doesn't give full name/address. Omit address. person info omitted.\n\nProduction locations: These are manufacturing locations. The schema has manufacturers[]. Should I add these as manufacturers? The fragment says \"Production location - The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\". These could be captured as manufacturers entries with name and address_lines. Or as matrix? The schema: manufacturers[]: name, address_lines[]. I'll add the four ABB entities as manufacturers. Hmm, but they are \"production locations\" — likely the manufacturer is ABB and production is at multiple sites. Given the schema, manufacturers[] seems the best fit. Alternatively, could put in a note. I'll include them as manufacturers entries.\n\nTables 9-12: These are matrix tables varying by configuration axis: two dimensions — bore (reduced vs full) and power supply (AC mains vs battery/renewable). Plus meter size.\n\nColumns: meter_size, indicating_range_maximum_value, indicating_range_minimum_value, verification_scale_interval_maximum_value. Units: mm, m³, m³, m³.\n\nNote decimal commas: 0,0001 → 0.0001, 0,001 → 0.001, 0,01 → 0.01, 0,1 → 0.1.\n\nI should emit matrix_tables for each of the four tables, plus config_level characteristics.\n\nFor characteristics.config_level: attributes varying by configuration. The config axes: bore (reduced/full) and power supply (AC mains / battery-renewable). E.g. attribute \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\", axis \"bore_and_power_supply\", values: [{condition: \"reduced bore, AC mains powered, DN40-DN65\", value: 0.0001, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}, ...]. That gets messy because value depends on both config and meter size (model). So really it's a 3-D thing: config (bore × power) × size → values. Best handled via matrix_tables per config, and model_level characteristics with model IDs like \"DN40 (reduced bore, AC mains)\".\n\nHmm. How to structure model_level? The models in this cert are meter sizes DN40...DN1800 across configs. The model_family.models could list configurations: e.g. model_id \"reduced_bore_ac_mains_DN40\"? That's a lot of entries. Alternatively, treat config as the model axis: models: [{\"model_id\": \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered\"}, ...] and config_level... no.\n\nGiven rule 5: config_level for attributes varying by configuration. Here values vary by meter size AND configuration. The pragmatic approach: use matrix_tables (4 tables) to preserve the data fully, and add config_level entries with conditions combining bore+power+size range, e.g.:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\",\n  \"axis\": \"bore_and_power_supply_by_meter_size\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN40–DN65\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN80\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n\nThat's very verbose. Perhaps simpler: keep the matrix tables as the canonical representation and include a compact config_level for indicating ranges too. But verbosity is acceptable — the instruction says to preserve multi-dim tables in matrix_tables that don't fit elsewhere. The 4 tables fit best in matrix_tables. I could also populate model_family models: meter sizes? Since the cert covers DN40–DN1800, models could be the meter sizes: model_id \"DN40\", ..., \"DN1800\". That's ~30 sizes. List them: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800. That's 29 sizes. All are mentioned in table 12 (DN1800 appears only in... let me check: Table 12 lists DN900-DN1650; DN1800 appears in Table 11? Table 11 full bore AC: DN40...DN300 — no DN1800. Table 12 battery: up to DN1650. Hmm, where is DN1800? Revision 5: \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\" Revision 6: \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800.\" But in these tables, DN1800 doesn't appear. Page 12 table continuation (Table 11 continued): DN350–DN600, DN700–DN1200, DN1350–DN1800 — yes! The continuation table on page 12 includes \"DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800\" — that's the continuation of Table 11 (Full bore – AC mains). Indeed the page 12 first table has no title but follows Table 11 and its rows: DN350-DN600 (0,1), DN700-DN1200 (0,1 with 99999999), DN1350-DN1800 (1 with 999999999). So Table 11 = full bore AC mains: DN40–DN1800.\n\nSo the meter sizes list: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800.\n\nFor model_level, meter size itself is the model identifier. Attributes like verification_scale_interval vary per (config, size). I think the cleanest is:\n\n- model_family.models: the configurations? Or sizes?\n\nGiven rule 4's examples, models are the variants. Here the variant axis is really \"meter size\" × \"configuration\". Since the tables group sizes with identical rows, models could be the row-groups per table. But that creates overlapping model definitions.\n\nI'll go with: matrix_tables for the 4 tables (fully preserving data), and config_level characteristics summarizing per attribute with conditions combining bore, power supply, and size groups. And model_family omitted or models = the configuration variants? Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: The schema's model_level is attribute × model. If I define models as the meter sizes (29 of them), then model_level attributes would need values per size per config — conflicting across configs. Not clean. So better: define models as the 4 configurations (which are genuinely different instrument variants: reduced/full bore, AC/battery powered). Then config axis = meter size, and config_level entries attribute × size. But config_level values: {condition, value} — condition would be the size group. That works:\n\nmodels: [\n  {\"model_id\": \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered\", \"attributes\": {\"bore\": \"reduced\", \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}},\n  {\"model_id\": \"Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\", ...},\n  {\"model_id\": \"Full bore – AC mains powered\", ...},\n  {\"model_id\": \"Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\", ...}\n]\n\nconfig_level: [\n  {\"attribute\": \"indicating_range_maximum_value\", \"axis\": \"meter_size\", \"model\": ..., \"values\": [...]},\n  ...\n]\n\nBut the config_level schema entry: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}. It doesn't have a \"model\" field... The values have {condition, value}. I can embed config in condition or in axis. Hmm. I could make four config_level entries per attribute, one per model, with axis \"meter size (reduced bore, AC mains)\". That's 4 attributes × 4 configs = 16 entries. Verbose but explicit.\n\nAlternatively, encode the condition as \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered, DN40–DN65\". That reduces to 4 config_level entries (one per attribute), each with grouped conditions. Values that repeat within group share the value. E.g.:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter size within bore and power supply configuration\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, AC mains, DN40–DN65\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, AC mains, DN80–DN200\", \"value\": 0.0001? ...}\n  ]\n}\n\nWait table 9: VSI: DN40-65: 0.0001; DN80: 0.0001; DN100-200: 0.001; DN250: 0.001; DN300-600: 0.01. Could group: DN40–DN80: 0.0001; DN100–DN250: 0.001; DN300–DN600: 0.01.\n\nTable 10 VSI: DN40-50: 0.0001; DN65: 0.001; DN80-150: 0.001; DN200: 0.01; DN250-450: 0.01; DN500-600: 0.1. Group: DN40–50: 0.0001; DN65–150: 0.001; DN200–450: 0.01; DN500–600: 0.1.\n\nTable 11 VSI: DN40-50: 0.0001; DN65: 0.0001; DN80-150: 0.001; DN200-300: 0.01; DN350-600: 0.1; DN700-1200: 0.1; DN1350-1800: 1. Group: DN40–65: 0.0001; DN80–150: 0.001; DN200–300: 0.01; DN350–1800: hmm DN350-1200: 0.1, DN1350-1800: 1.\n\nTable 12 VSI: DN40-50: 0.001; DN65-125: 0.001; DN150: 0.01; DN200-500: 0.01; DN600: 0.1; DN700-800: 0.1; DN900-1650: 1.\n\nThis is getting complex. Honestly, the matrix_tables preserve everything faithfully. I'll include both: matrix_tables (4 tables, with the page-12 continuation merged into Table 11) and a set of config_level entries. Given the complexity, maybe config_level with one entry per attribute, conditions = combination of config + size group. Let me draft:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n\n1. attribute: indicating_range_maximum_value, axis: \"meter size within bore type and power supply\", values: many conditions. Indicating max: 9999999 for most; 99999999 for full-bore-AC DN700–1200; 999999999 for full-bore-AC DN1350–1800; battery-full-bore: 99999999 for DN600 and DN700-800; 999999999 for DN900–1650.\n\n2. indicating_range_minimum_value: various.\n\n3. verification_scale_interval_maximum_value: as above.\n\nAlternatively, I can skip config_level since matrix_tables capture it, per schema note: \"matrix_tables[]: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". These are 3-D (bore × power × size), which don't fit cleanly elsewhere → matrix_tables is the designated home. I'll include matrix_tables as primary and also provide model_level? Hmm.\n\nLet me decide: I'll provide matrix_tables (4, with the continuation merged into table 11 with a note), and config_level with compact grouped conditions for the three attributes. Actually, to balance, I'll do config_level with one entry per attribute per table (12 entries), where axis names the config, and values grouped by size ranges. That's explicit and traceable:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter size (reduced bore, AC mains powered)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40–DN80\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN100–DN250\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN300–DN600\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nThat's 4 tables × 3 attributes = 12 entries. Each with 3-5 groups. Reasonable.\n\nAlso indicating_range_maximum_value groups:\n\nTable 9 (reduced, AC): all DN40–DN600: 9999999. One group.\nTable 10 (reduced, battery): DN40–DN600: 9999999. One group.\nTable 11 (full, AC): DN40–DN600: 9999999; DN700–DN1200: 99999999; DN1350–DN1800: 999999999.\nTable 12 (full, battery): DN40–DN500: 9999999; DN600–DN800: 99999999; DN900–DN1650: 999999999.\n\nIndicating min:\n\nTable 9: DN40–65: 99999; DN80–600: 999999. Wait: DN80: 999999; DN100-200: 999999; DN250: 9999999; DN300-600: 9999999. So groups: DN40–DN65: 99999; DN80–DN200: 999999; DN250–DN600: 9999999.\n\nTable 10: DN40–50: 99999; DN65–150: 999999; DN200–450: 9999999; DN500–600: 9999999 → DN200–600: 9999999. So: DN40–DN50: 99999; DN65–DN150: 999999; DN200–DN600: 9999999.\n\nTable 11: DN40–50: 99999; DN65–150: 999999; DN200–300: 9999999; DN350–600: 9999999 → DN200–600: 9999999; DN700–1200: 99999999; DN1350–1800: 99999999 (min value 99999999, max 999999999). So: DN40–DN50: 99999; DN65–DN150: 999999; DN200–DN600: 9999999; DN700–DN1800: 99999999.\n\nTable 12: DN40–50: 99999; DN65–150: 999999; DN200–500: 9999999; DN600: 9999999 → DN200–600: 9999999; DN700–800: 99999999; DN900–1650: 99999999. So: DN40–DN50: 99999; DN65–DN150: 999999; DN200–DN600: 9999999; DN700–DN1650: 99999999.\n\nVSI:\n\nTable 9: DN40–DN80: 0.0001; DN100–DN250: 0.001; DN300–DN600: 0.01.\nTable 10: DN40–DN50: 0.0001; DN65–DN150: 0.001; DN200–DN450: 0.01; DN500–DN600: 0.1.\nTable 11: DN40–DN65: 0.0001; DN80–DN150: 0.001; DN200–DN300: 0.01; DN350–DN1200: 0.1; DN1350–DN1800: 1.\nTable 12: DN40–DN125: 0.001; DN150–DN500: 0.01; DN600–DN800: 0.1; DN900–DN1650: 1.\n\nOK.\n\nNow matrix_tables: I'll name them and include columns with units. The schema example includes \"column_units\" even though the base schema lines list name, columns[], rows[], description, footnotes[] — the example adds column_units, so it's allowed.\n\nColumns: [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_maximum_value\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\"], units [mm, m³, m³, m³].\n\nRows: each row object: {\"meter_size\": \"DN40, DN50, DN65\", ...}? Or expand sizes? The example R137 used one row per size. Here rows group multiple sizes. Options: (a) keep grouped rows as in source: meter_size: \"DN40, DN50, DN65\"; (b) expand into one row per size. Expanding creates 29+ rows per table ×4 = ~100 rows. Grouped keeps fidelity. I think keeping the grouped rows as in the source is fine and faithful; the rows array allows objects. But for machine consumption, maybe expand? The rule says preserve tables; source rows are grouped. I'll keep source grouping but maybe expand for usability? Hmm. Expanding makes rows unambiguous. Table 9: sizes DN40,DN50,DN65 → 3 rows; DN80 →1; DN100,DN125,DN150,DN200 →4; DN250 →1; DN300,350,400,450,500,600 →6. Total 16 rows. Table 10: 2+1+4+1+5+2=15 rows. Table 11: 2+1+4+3+6+8+6=30 rows. Table 12: 2+4+1+7+1+3+10=28 rows. Total ~89 rows. That's a lot of JSON but fine. Actually, maybe keep grouped to be concise and faithful. The instruction \"Don't fabricate\" — expanding is not fabrication, just normalization. I'll expand each size into its own row — clearer for downstream. Hmm, but 89 rows... It's fine; JSON can handle it. Actually let me reconsider: grouping preserves exactly the source; expanding aids per-model extraction. The example in rule 4 expanded per size. I'll expand.\n\nWait, also for Table 11 continuation on page 12: rows DN350–DN600, DN700–DN1200, DN1350–DN1800. Merge into table 11. I'll note in description that the table spans pages 11–12.\n\nAlso model_family: include? The cert covers multiple variants (bore × power × size). family_name: null? The products are water meters (R49) — ABB AquaMaster (mentioned in revision 9: \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"). But that's a specific variant of calculating and indicating device added in rev 9. I could set family_name null. models: the 4 configurations? I think including model_family with the 4 configuration variants makes sense, each with attributes {bore, power_supply}.\n\nAlternatively omit model_family since sizes are the models... The guidance: include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant — it does. I'll include with 4 configuration models plus attributes bore/power_supply.\n\nHmm, but sizes are also variants. I could include meter sizes as a config axis instead. I'll do:\n\nmodel_family: {\n  \"family_name\": null,\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered\", \"label\": \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered\", \"attributes\": {\"bore\": \"reduced\", \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}},\n    {\"model_id\": \"Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\", ...},\n    {\"model_id\": \"Full bore – AC mains powered\", ...},\n    {\"model_id\": \"Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\", ...}\n  ]\n}\n\nSince this is a fragment, should I include certificate header info? Yes — the fragment shows header on each page: Number, Project number, page X of 13, member state. Include:\n\ncertificate: {\n  number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n  scheme: \"A\",\n  project_number: \"4076767\",\n  page_total: 13,\n  member_state: \"The Netherlands\",\n  revision: \"15\"? — schema has revision_history; certificate number includes \"revision 15\". I could add certificate.revision? Not in schema; skip, but revision_history covers it.\n  date_issued: from revision history, revision 15 dated 16 February 2026 → that's the current revision date, i.e., issuance. Include date_issued: \"2026-02-16\"? The fragment doesn't explicitly say \"date issued\" but the revision table gives revision 15 date 16 February 2026, and the header says revision 15. It's reasonable to infer date_issued = 2026-02-16. Rule: don't fabricate — but this is a direct derivation. I'll include it. Hmm, risky? It's a reasonable inference. I'll include.\n  oiml_issuer_id: not present (NL1? The number contains \"NL1\" which might be the issuer code). Actually \"NL1\" in \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — NMi's issuer ID? Hmm. OIML issuer IDs are like \"NMi\" or numeric? The cert number segment \"NL1\" could be country+issuer. I'd leave oiml_issuer_id omitted since not explicitly stated.\n}\n\nrecommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [\"1\"]}. Should amendment be included? Not mentioned. accuracy_classes: only class 1 mentioned in rev 6 (\"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800\"). Hmm, R49 accuracy classes are 1 and 2. The fragment mentions only class 1. Include [\"1\"]? Or omit accuracy_classes since the full list isn't given? The fragment's evidence: class 1 exists. I'll include [\"1\"] — hmm, but that could imply the cert is only class 1, which is false (cert certainly covers class 2 from initial issue). Since I can't verify class 2 from the fragment, including [\"1\"] is accurate to the fragment. Actually \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800\" implies class 1 was added for DN1800 specifically, meaning other sizes had class 1 earlier. Doesn't mention class 2 at all. I'll include accuracy_classes: [\"1\"] — wait, \"E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter\" — in R49, flow meters have meter classes? Actually R49 defines accuracy classes 1 and 2 for water meters. E2/O... Hmm, in R49-1 there are \"metrological classes\" ... no. Let me think: R49 (2013) hmm. Actually I recall R49-1:2013 defines accuracy classes 1 and 2. Temperature classes: T30, T50... \"E2\" hmm. For heat meters (R75) there are environmental classes A/B/C. \"E2\" might be electromagnetic class E2 (R49-2 test conditions define electromagnetic environments E1, E2, E3?). Yes! R49-2 defines electromagnetic environment classes E1, E2, E3 and... hmm \"O class\"? Actually R49 defines \"class O\" hmm. I'm not sure. I'll not include E2/O in accuracy_classes since they're ambiguous; only class 1.\n\nActually, maybe safer to omit accuracy_classes entirely? The rules say extract what's present. Class 1 is present. I'll go with [\"1\"].\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\". Address not in fragment. Omit other fields. Should I include oiml_issuer_id? No.\n\nmanufacturers: The production locations. Names: \"ABB Limited\", \"ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd.\", \"ABB Inc.\", \"ABB India Limited\". Addresses:\n- ABB Limited: \"Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom (UK)\" → address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]. Keep \"(UK)\"? I'll normalize: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom (UK)\"]. Fine.\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd.: [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway\", \"Pudong New District, Shanghai\", \"201315, P.R. China\"] — original: \"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201315, P.R. China.\" I'll split: [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway\", \"Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201315\", \"P.R. China\"].\n- ABB Inc.: [\"125 E. County Line Road\", \"Warminster, PA 18974\", \"United States of America (USA)\"].\n- ABB India Limited: [\"Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area\", \"Bengaluru – 560058\", \"Karnataka India\"] — original: \"Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area, Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka India.\" Split: [\"Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area\", \"Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka\", \"India\"]. Fine.\n\nShould these be manufacturers or something else? The schema has manufacturers[] — best fit. Add note? There's no note field. OK.\n\ncertified_type: category \"Water meter\" (R49 water meters). type_designations: from fragment? \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" mentioned in revision history (a variant of calculating and indicating device). Hmm — that's mentioned in revision 9 description. Should I include as type designation? It's a device variant name. The certified type is a water meter (family AquaMaster). Including \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" as a type designation is grounded in the fragment. But it's specifically \"variant of calculating and indicating device\". Maybe include in components? Not clearly. I'll omit certified_type.type_designations or include [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]? Hmm, risk of misrepresenting. The fragment doesn't have a certified type section. Rule 8: omit if not present. But category can be inferred from R49 = water meters (\"The water meter is produced at...\"). The text explicitly says \"water meter\", so category: \"Water meter\" is grounded. type_designations: I'll omit — or include the AquaMaster4 mention? I'll leave type_designations out to avoid noise. Hmm, actually including it with the context could help. The schema allows components[] \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\". The fragment names \"calculating and indicating device\" variant AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z and \"flow transmitter\" (rev 3) and \"U200 wireless module\" (rev 10) and \"(virtual) full bore sensor\" (rev 2). These are sub-assemblies named in revision descriptions. But they're mentioned as change descriptions, not as certified configuration. I'll skip components to avoid fabrication-by-context.\n\ntest_reports: none in fragment.\n\nrevision_history: from the table — revision \"Initial\" date 2020-08-14, then 1..15. changes as given. Note dates: revision 11 \"27 January 2024\" (out of order vs rev 10 26 February 2024 — that's as printed; keep as-is). Revision 14: \"8 January 2026\" Documentation folder update. Revision 15: 16 February 2026 Software update.\n\nConvert dates to ISO: \n- Initial: 2020-08-14\n- 1: 2020-09-30\n- 2: 2020-12-24\n- 3: 2021-08-02\n- 4: 2021-08-26\n- 5: 2021-11-23\n- 6: 2022-05-20\n- 7: 2022-08-30\n- 8: 2023-07-03\n- 9: 2023-09-23\n- 10: 2024-02-26\n- 11: 2024-01-27\n- 12: 2025-04-07\n- 13: 2025-06-05\n- 14: 2026-01-08\n- 15: 2026-02-16\n\nrevision format string: \"Initial\", \"1\", ..., \"15\".\n\nchanges text: keep as-is, cleaning the \"– 25 °C\" → \"-25 °C\" fine. Revision 3 changes combine two lines separated by <br>: \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to – 25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\" I'll join with space. Revision 9: \"Addition of AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating